What survives:
(a) Distinct new paths merge cleanly. X-01.F20 is a local scratch test on git 2.56.0.windows.4: squash and normal merges of distinct event files had no conflict, a shared append file conflicted, and merge=union kept both lines in random order (E-09.F07, X-01.F21), so no merge driver is better. The system gitconfig sets pull.rebase=false, and D syncs with an explicit --no-rebase, so sync is a merge. `* -text` is needed and enough against core.autocrlf=true (system gitconfig) to keep the bytes equal to the name hash (X-01.F24).
(b) The entry-state fold is order-independent IF it is a pure re-fold per entry over events sorted by (lamport, event_id). Architect A says ingest uses INSERT OR IGNORE and then "refolds touched entries". Event ids are sha256 of canonical bytes, so the order is total, and the toy fold gave 1 distinct state over 2000 shuffled ingest orders. Fossil (E-09.F05) and git-bug (E-09.F13) are production precedent for rebuilding state from immutable artifacts with a deterministic order.
(c) Batch commits are cheap on this box. These are proxies, not git commit:
- writing 300 events (tmp+rename into events/<aa>/) took 0.56-0.61 s;
- simulated loose objects for 300 blobs plus about 190 trees and a commit took 0.90-0.93 s;
- 1,000 events on top of 20,000 prior took 1.72 s plus 1.86 s;
- fsync on each of 560 objects took 1.19 s against 1.14 s without;
- git hash-object --stdin-paths (no -w) hashed 1,000 files in 0.21 s;
- git --no-optional-locks status over 677 tracked files took 0.13 s.
Tree write amplification grows with store size: at 12,000 prior events a 300-event commit writes about 0.6 MB of compressed trees against 47 KB of blobs. That is bounded, and git gc packs loose objects automatically once there are about 6,700 (gc.auto). All of this is far below the 10 s falsifier. Caveat: Get-MpComputerStatus reports Defender real-time protection off, so a scanner would raise these numbers.

narrowed_claim:
Distinct, well-formed event files merge across the per-host clones as pure adds with no merge driver. Well-formed means: bytes hash to the name, canonical JSON, `* -text`, no tmp files committed. "Across worktrees" is not a merge at all: every worktree on a host shares one store working tree, and the commit lease serialises git there. The ENTRY-STATE fold is ingest-order independent provided:
- it re-folds each entry from scratch over events sorted by (lamport, event_id), which is a total order because ids are content hashes;
- it is run by one pinned CLI build (schema, recipe_version, runner_version, scrub_ruleset_sha).
The INDEX as a whole is identical across ingest orders, hosts and restores only if:
- every ranked or shown list ends in a unique key such as entry_id;
- rebuild ingests in sorted order;
- the index is keyed by, or rebuilt on, every code-version input as well as the schema.
Phase-close batches of a few hundred events cost about 1-2 s of file and object I/O on this Windows box (proxy, Defender off; real git add+commit not yet measured), and object growth stays bounded under auto-gc.
